WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:07.079
A young prince wrestles with a dilemma: whether or not to take revenge when his uncle seizes the throne.

2
00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:12.521
This is the story of Hamlet, perhaps William Shakespeare's most famous play.

3
00:00:12.522 --> 00:00:22.159
It begins on a bitterly cold night at Elsinore Castle in Denmark, where two guards, Barnardo and Marcellus, are keeping watch.

4
00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:27.869
"Who's there?" called Marcellus warily. He had spotted someone in the darkness.

5
00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:31.530
"It is I, Horatio," the man replied.

6
00:00:31.531 --> 00:00:40.793
As Horatio approached, Barnardo explained to Marcellus, "I asked him to keep watch with us tonight, in case that thing reappears.

7
00:00:40.794 --> 00:00:44.597
It seems right, since he is Prince Hamlet's friend."

8
00:00:44.605 --> 00:00:49.373
"Come now, it's surely just your imaginations," said Horatio.

9
00:00:49.374 --> 00:00:54.389
"But we've seen it two nights in a row," insisted Barnardo, "and—"

10
00:00:54.390 --> 00:01:00.109
"Shh," interrupted Marcellus, his eyes widening. "Here it comes again!"

11
00:01:00.110 --> 00:01:03.728
A ghostly figure hovered in the night mist.

12
00:01:03.729 --> 00:01:11.924
Overcoming his fear, Horatio faced the phantom and called out, "Speak, and tell us what kind of thing you are!"

13
00:01:11.925 --> 00:01:17.195
But the ghost turned away and melted into the night. Horatio went pale.

14
00:01:17.196 --> 00:01:22.361
"I would not believe this if I hadn't seen it with my own eyes," he murmured.

15
00:01:22.362 --> 00:01:27.021
"It looks like the old king—may he rest in peace," Barnardo said.

16
00:01:27.022 --> 00:01:34.328
"Yes. And it was wearing the same armor our king wore when he slew the king of Norway," replied Horatio.

17
00:01:34.329 --> 00:01:38.631
"This ghost may be a sign that violence is coming to Denmark."

18
00:01:38.632 --> 00:01:45.543
"Well, our whole country seems to be preparing for war," said Marcellus. "Do you know why?"

19
00:01:45.545 --> 00:01:54.082
"Because young Fortinbras, the Norwegian king's son, wants to take back the lands that his father lost to us," Horatio explained.

20
00:01:54.083 --> 00:02:00.622
"He's gathered an army. Perhaps that's what's troubling the spirit of the dead king . . . Look!"

21
00:02:00.623 --> 00:02:05.269
The ghost had appeared again, its arms spread wide.

22
00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:11.192
"Stay!" Horatio shouted. "If you know something about our country's fate, speak!"

23
00:02:11.193 --> 00:02:16.198
Just then a rooster crowed, and the apparition faded away.

24
00:02:16.199 --> 00:02:26.197
"Day is dawning," said Horatio. "Spirits don't like the light. Come, we must tell Prince Hamlet that we've seen his father's ghost!"

25
00:02:26.198 --> 00:02:32.861
That morning the new king of Denmark, Claudius, had gathered his court in the castle's great hall.

26
00:02:32.862 --> 00:02:38.385
Claudius was the brother of the old king, who had died only two months earlier.

27
00:02:38.386 --> 00:02:44.199
Claudius had quickly married Gertrude, the king's widow, and so had become king.

28
00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:50.983
"I am sending ambassadors to Norway," Claudius announced, "to discuss the Fortinbras situation."

29
00:02:50.984 --> 00:02:55.722
The king then nodded to Hamlet. "My nephew and now my son—"

30
00:02:55.723 --> 00:03:00.501
"I am not your son," Hamlet muttered under his breath.

31
00:03:00.502 --> 00:03:10.306
"—why do you look so sad?" continued the king. "I know you mourn your father, but everyone must die sometime. Too much grief is not manly.

32
00:03:10.307 --> 00:03:16.261
I realize you want to return to your studies in Germany, but I would prefer you stay in Elsinore."

33
00:03:16.262 --> 00:03:20.238
"Yes," said Queen Gertrude, "please stay, Hamlet."

34
00:03:20.239 --> 00:03:28.315
"I shall obey you, madam," Hamlet replied politely, but he was miserable. "I cannot understand my mother," he thought.

35
00:03:28.323 --> 00:03:35.838
"I believed she truly loved my father, who was far nobler, fairer, and braver than Claudius.

36
00:03:35.839 --> 00:03:41.342
How could she dishonor my father's memory by so quickly marrying my uncle?"

37
00:03:41.342 --> 00:03:44.186
Just then Horatio entered the hall.

38
00:03:44.187 --> 00:03:48.057
"Horatio," said Hamlet, "I am very glad to see you!"

39
00:03:48.057 --> 00:03:57.240
Horatio drew Hamlet aside. "My lord," he whispered, "This will shock you. I saw your father's ghost last night."

40
00:03:57.241 --> 00:04:04.730
"What?" Astonished, Hamlet questioned his friend and then agreed to join the guards and Horatio that evening.

41
00:04:04.731 --> 00:04:09.707
"My father's spirit, in armor!" he thought. "All is not well!"

42
00:04:09.708 --> 00:04:17.897
In another part of the castle, Laertes, the son of Polonius, Claudius' chief adviser, was preparing to go to France.

43
00:04:17.898 --> 00:04:22.284
He went to say good-bye to his sister, Ophelia, in her sewing room.

44
00:04:22.285 --> 00:04:25.593
"Remember to write to me, dear sister," he said.

45
00:04:25.594 --> 00:04:27.896
"Of course," she replied.

46
00:04:27.897 --> 00:04:36.987
"One more thing," he added. "I know Hamlet's been courting you, but I wouldn't encourage him. I don't know if he's serious about you."

47
00:04:36.988 --> 00:04:42.863
Ophelia bristled at her brother's words, but before she could reply, their father entered.

48
00:04:42.864 --> 00:04:47.720
Polonius had a long gray beard and thought himself quite wise.

49
00:04:47.721 --> 00:04:52.466
"Laertes!" he said, "before you leave, I have some advice for you.

50
00:04:52.467 --> 00:05:00.263
Always think before you act. Do not wear clothes that are too fancy. Neither borrow nor lend . . ."

51
00:05:00.264 --> 00:05:07.716
The old man went on for several minutes and concluded with, "And above all, to thine own self be true."

52
00:05:07.717 --> 00:05:15.161
"I must go, Father! Ophelia, remember what I said to you," called Laertes as he strode out the door.

53
00:05:15.162 --> 00:05:18.415
"What did he say?" asked Polonius.

54
00:05:18.416 --> 00:05:21.581
"It was about Prince Hamlet," said Ophelia.

55
00:05:21.582 --> 00:05:28.999
"Ah yes." The old man nodded. "I hear he's been spending a lot of time with you. What is going on?"

56
00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:31.553
"He told me he loves me, Father."

57
00:05:31.554 --> 00:05:37.727
"Hmph," said Polonius. "Beware of young men! Do not believe his vows.

58
00:05:37.728 --> 00:05:40.847
And do not waste any more time with him."

59
00:05:40.848 --> 00:05:46.571
Ophelia looked down. "I shall obey, my lord," she said reluctantly.

60
00:05:46.572 --> 00:05:51.981
That night Hamlet, Horatio, and Marcellus gathered on the guards' platform.

61
00:05:51.982 --> 00:05:54.496
"What time is it?" asked Hamlet.

62
00:05:54.497 --> 00:06:00.326
"Just past midnight," Marcellus replied. "The time the spirit usually appears."

63
00:06:00.327 --> 00:06:03.802
"Look!" shouted Horatio, "there it is!"

64
00:06:03.803 --> 00:06:13.244
Hamlet stared as the ghostly figure materialized. "Angels defend us!" he cried. "Are you a good spirit or a demon? Answer me!"

65
00:06:13.245 --> 00:06:16.386
The ghost silently beckoned Hamlet to follow him.

66
00:06:16.387 --> 00:06:19.593
"Don't go," whispered Marcellus fearfully.

67
00:06:19.593 --> 00:06:23.799
"I'm not afraid," declared Hamlet, stepping toward the ghost.

68
00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:28.497
"What if it's evil?" said Horatio. "What if it means you harm?"

69
00:06:28.498 --> 00:06:34.982
"Stay!" cried Marcellus. He grabbed Hamlet's right arm. Horatio grabbed his left.

70
00:06:34.983 --> 00:06:43.019
"Let go of me!" roared Hamlet, shaking himself free. To the ghost, he called out, "Go on. I'll follow you."

71
00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:48.726
As Hamlet strode after the ghost, Horatio said, "I'm worried. Let's go after him."

72
00:06:48.727 --> 00:06:55.029
Marcellus nodded. "Something is rotten in the state of Denmark," he muttered.

73
00:06:55.029 --> 00:06:57.029
The ghost led Hamlet away.

74
00:06:57.031 --> 00:07:07.583
"I am your father's spirit," it said, "doomed to walk the night and spend my days consumed by fire until my sins are burned away.

75
00:07:07.584 --> 00:07:13.553
Oh Hamlet, if you ever did love me, revenge my terrible murder!"

76
00:07:13.554 --> 00:07:17.016
"Murder?" exclaimed Hamlet, shocked.

77
00:07:17.017 --> 00:07:20.571
"Yes, murder," groaned the ghost.

78
00:07:20.572 --> 00:07:25.804
"People say I was bitten by a snake, but that is false!

79
00:07:25.805 --> 00:07:31.772
The serpent that stung me now wears my crown and has married my wife.

80
00:07:31.773 --> 00:07:38.088
While I was sleeping one day in the garden, your uncle poured a deadly poison in my ear.

81
00:07:38.089 --> 00:07:48.353
It quickly flowed through my blood and killed me. And so my brother deprived me all at once of life, of crown, of queen.

82
00:07:48.354 --> 00:07:59.297
He ended my life before I could confess my sins and purify my soul! Hamlet, you must avenge my death! Farewell, remember me."

83
00:07:59.298 --> 00:08:04.678
The ghost vanished into the mist just as Horatio and Marcellus joined Hamlet.

84
00:08:04.679 --> 00:08:07.789
"What happened, my lord?" cried Horatio.

85
00:08:07.790 --> 00:08:14.278
Hamlet just stood there, still stunned. "You must swear to keep it secret," he finally said.

86
00:08:14.279 --> 00:08:17.976
"Swear!" cried the ghost from the darkness.

87
00:08:17.977 --> 00:08:24.691
They all shuddered. The two men swore secrecy, and Hamlet recounted what the ghost had said.

88
00:08:24.692 --> 00:08:30.373
"From now on," said Hamlet, "I'll pretend to be a little crazy.

89
00:08:30.374 --> 00:08:34.611
Then my uncle won't suspect I'm seeking revenge.

90
00:08:34.612 --> 00:08:40.869
These are terrible times, and I dread what I must do to set them right!"

